    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the project, and it passes along with the full build.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required that a successful owner create enqueue a welcome notification, emitted via the logger named `NOTIFY` on a line carrying the owner id and the `memberId`.\n\n**Change** (`OwnerAuditListener.java` only):\n- Added a `NOTIFY` logger field.\n- Added one line to the existing `@PostPersist onCreate` handler:\n  ```java\n  NOTIFY.info(\"welcome ownerId={} memberId={}\", owner.getId(), owner.getMemberId());\n  ```\n\n**Why this placement keeps structural impact minimal:** `onCreate` already fires exactly on first-persist (successful create) and already has the owner id and memberId in hand. The alternatives \u2014 editing the large `addOwner` controller method (high CC inside a class with large WMC_other) or adding a new listener class (forcing an edit to the big `Owner` entity's `@EntityListeners` and doubling the file-count multiplier) \u2014 both cost more. Here I touched one file and one tiny single-method class: the changed function has CC 1 and WMC_other of 0, so the added complexity is essentially the single log line itself.\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.",
